  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}(2+2x-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}A+{\u222c}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}A-{\u222c}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is a constant, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}A=2\\,\\text{Area}(R)=2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2)=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}A & =(2-0){\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2({3}^{2}-(0)^{2}) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the third integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}A & =(3-0){\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3({2}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}(2+2x-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=12+18-24=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
